<section class="project-overview" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
    <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-section-label">Overview</p>
        <h2 id="overview-title">Combining music and productivity in one platform</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="project-section-content">
        <p>
            Calico was built as a group project during the Northcoders Enterprise
            Engineering Bootcamp. The goal was to combine music discovery with
            productivity tools in a single application, giving the team a chance to
            design and build a full-stack product together rather than working solo.
        </p>
        <p>
            Users can search for and play LoFi music, register and log in, and run
            a task timer or a 25-minute Pomodoro session alongside the music. The
            Blazor frontend communicates with an ASP.NET Core Web API over HTTP,
            which uses Entity Framework Core for data access, while Supabase
            handles user authentication and issues the tokens the API uses to
            protect its endpoints.
        </p>
        <p>
            Playlists and organising timed sessions by project are part of the
            original design and already modelled in the database, but the UI for
            them isn't built yet — that's next on the list to finish.
        </p>
        <p>
            Since the group project, I've gone back to Calico independently —
            carrying out a full security review, fixing what it found, and
            deploying the finished application.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="project-contribution" aria-labelledby="contribution-title">
    <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-section-label">My contribution</p>
        <h2 id="contribution-title">How I contributed to Calico</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="contribution-grid">
        <article class="contribution-card">
            <p class="contribution-label">Collaboration</p>
            <h3>Planning and delivering as a team</h3>
            <p>
                I worked primarily through pair programming, taking part as both driver
                and navigator across frontend and backend tasks. We held daily stand-ups,
                managed our tasks with Trello and reviewed and merged our Git changes
                together at the end of each day.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="contribution-card">
            <p class="contribution-label">Individual work</p>
            <h3>Pair-programmed features and independent work</h3>
            <p>
                As navigator, I contributed to the user account settings page and paired on
                the YouTube feature across its backend, frontend, API integration and search
                page. Independently, I implemented the API and database health checks and
                built the initial Pomodoro timer prototype.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="contribution-card">
            <p class="contribution-label">Since the bootcamp</p>
            <h3>Independent security review and bug fixes</h3>
            <p>
                After the bootcamp, I went back to Calico independently and ran a full
                security review of the backend. I fixed what it found, including missing
                authorisation checks that let any logged-in user view or edit someone
                else's projects and account, and a mass-assignment flaw that let users
                self-promote to admin. I also restored the real YouTube search
                integration, which the group had replaced with a hardcoded list of
                results because of API quota limits.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="contribution-card">
            <p class="contribution-label">Since the bootcamp</p>
            <h3>Fixing the frontend end-to-end</h3>
            <p>
                I then fixed the frontend, where login, signup and the dashboard had
                never worked correctly end-to-end. I rebuilt login and signup to work
                properly with Supabase, connected the dashboard to the real logged-in
                user instead of a placeholder, added a working logout, and merged in
                and fixed an unfinished Pomodoro timer.
            </p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

