<section class="project-integrations" aria-labelledby="integrations-title">
    <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-section-label">Integrations</p>
        <h2 id="integrations-title">Connecting external services and application layers</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="integrations-grid">
        <article class="integration-card">
            <h3>Supabase authentication</h3>
            <p>
                User accounts and login are handled by Supabase, which issues a JWT
                on sign-in. The backend verifies each token's signature against
                Supabase's public keys and uses its claims to identify the caller
                and enforce ownership and admin checks on every protected endpoint.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="integration-card">
            <h3>YouTube media</h3>
            <p>
                I worked as the navigator while my pair and I built the backend, frontend,
                API integration and a dedicated YouTube search page. With limited time before
                the demo, we fell back to a hardcoded list of results shown on the home page
                instead of the real API, due to YouTube quota limits. After the bootcamp, I
                restored the real API integration with a caching layer to manage quota, and
                decided to keep search permanently merged into the home page rather than
                reviving the separate page.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="integration-card">
            <h3>Frontend and API communication</h3>
            <p>
                The Blazor Server frontend runs entirely on its own server, so
                every request to the backend happens server-side through a
                centrally configured HttpClient rather than directly from the
                browser. Since the login token lives in a cookie set by the
                frontend rather than being forwarded automatically, each request
                attaches it manually as a Bearer token so the backend can verify
                who's calling.
            </p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="project-deployment" aria-labelledby="deployment-title">
    <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-section-label">Deployment</p>
        <h2 id="deployment-title">Taking the application from local development to production</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="deployment-content">
        <p>
            I used Docker to package the frontend and backend, tested each
            container locally to make sure it worked the same way it would in
            production, and deployed them to Railway as individual services. I
            also set up a GitHub Actions pipeline
            for each project, so every push automatically builds and checks
            that nothing is broken before it goes anywhere near production.
        </p>
        <p>
            The backend uses SQLite for local development and an Azure SQL
            Database in production, with separate configuration for each so
            the right settings are picked up automatically. The frontend is
            available through a custom subdomain of my portfolio domain.
        </p>

        <ul class="deployment-tools" aria-label="Deployment tools used">
            <li>Docker</li>
            <li>Railway</li>
            <li>Azure SQL</li>
            <li>GitHub Actions</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="project-reflection" aria-labelledby="reflection-title">
    <div class="reflection-content">
        <p class="project-section-label">Reflection</p>
        <h2 id="reflection-title">What I developed through this project</h2>

        <p>
            Working on Calico strengthened my experience of pair programming in
            both driver and navigator roles and contributing to a shared full-stack codebase.
            Daily stand-ups, Trello planning and end-of-day Git reviews and merges
            taught me how a development team coordinates work, shares knowledge and
            delivers features together. Going back to Calico independently afterwards
            taught me a different set of skills — carrying out a proper security
            review, working through what it found methodically, and taking an
            application all the way from a group project to something genuinely
            deployed and working end-to-end.
        </p>
    </div>

